      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the solutions to the equations \\(x + y = 4\\) and \\(3xy = 4\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\):**\n\n   From the equation \\(x + y = 4\\), we can solve for \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   y = 4 - x\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute \\(y\\) into the second equation:**\n\n   Substitute \\(y = 4 - x\\) into the equation \\(3xy = 4\\):\n   \\[\n   3x(4 - x) = 4\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the resulting equation:**\n\n   Expand and simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   12x - 3x^2 = 4\n   \\]\n   Rearrange all terms to one side of the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   3x^2 - 12x + 4 = 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:**\n\n   The quadratic formula is given by:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = -12\\), and \\(c = 4\\). Substitute these values into the formula:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}}{2 \\cdot 3}\n   \\]\n   Simplify inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 48}}{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{96}}{6}\n   \\]\n   Simplify \\(\\sqrt{96}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{96} = \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 6} = 4\\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   Substitute back:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{12 \\pm 4\\sqrt{6}}{6}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the fraction:\n   \\[\n   x = 2 \\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Express the solutions in the required form:**\n\n   The solutions are:\n   \\[\n   x = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Identify the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\):**\n\n   The solutions are in the form \\(x = \\frac{a \\pm b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{6 \\pm 6\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{6 - 6\\sqrt{6}}{3} = 2 - 2\\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a = 6\\), \\(b = 6\\), \\(c = 6\\), and \\(d = 3\\).\n\n7. **Calculate \\(a + b + c + 